Consider filling Basket A first. For this, you have five fruit and must choose three of them. There are 5 choices for the first fruit, and once this is chosen there are 4 for the next fruit and finally 3 choices for the last fruit and so $5\cdot 4\cdot 3$ choices. Since the order of the fruit going in to the basket is irrelevant one must divide through the number of ways of ordering the 3 fruit to avoid overcounting. There are $3!$ ways to order these three fruits (namely the number of permutations of three elements) and so there are
\begin{align*}
\frac{5\cdot 4\cdot 3}{3!} = \frac{5!}{3!2!}=\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
choices. For Basket B, there are only two remaining fruit and both must go into the basket and so there are no extra choices and so this is the final answer.

